 "literal": "Small white water-crow's red mouth, large flock / throwing wave's spray wet back making wet, / cold disliking staying many-flowered seashore grove / expanding water's lord of the seashore if he leaves, our / sweet life other than another thing / what indeed, friend, we losing?",
 "translation": "O friend! If the lord of the seashore of expanding waters—where large flocks of small, white water-crows with red mouths stay, disliking the cold, their wet backs drenched by the spray of throwing waves in the seashore grove of many flowers—leaves us, what else is there for us to lose other than our own sweet life?",
 "modern": "சிறிய வெண்ணிற நீர்க்காக்கைகளின் சிவந்த வாயைக் கொண்ட பெரிய கூட்டம், வீசுகின்ற அலையின் துளிகள் தம்முடைய குளிர்ந்த முதுகை நனைக்க, குளிரை வெறுத்துத் தங்குகின்ற பல பூக்களைக் கொண்ட கடற்கரைச் சோலைக்குத் தலைவனான விரிந்த நீரையுடைய சேர்ப்பன் நம்மை விட்டுப் பிரிந்தால், தோழியே! நம்முடைய இனிய உயிரைத் தவிர நாம் இழப்பதற்கு வேறு என்ன இருக்கிறது?",
 "explanation": "The speaker is the heroine talking to her confidante in a neytal (seashore) landscape. The hero has been delaying their marriage, and the heroine expresses her deep anguish at the thought of his separation. She describes the seashore grove where small white water-crows with red mouths huddle together to avoid the cold spray of the waves. This image of the birds seeking comfort together in the cold seashore grove contrasts with her own lonely state, highlighting her vulnerability. She tells her friend that if the lord of this shore leaves her, she has nothing left to lose but her very life. Key words include 'சேர்ப்பன்' (lord of the seashore), 'இன்னுயிர்' (sweet life), and 'இழப்பது' (losing).",
